WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.828
Task statement 1.5.

2
00:00:01.828 --> 00:00:06.861
Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization.

3
00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:18.185
When three backend tools report dates in three formats, Claude will eventually misread one, and a refund cap written only in the prompt will eventually be broken.

4
00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:23.407
Hooks are checkpoints that run your own code at fixed moments.

5
00:00:23.407 --> 00:00:27.168
One runs before a tool call, where you can allow or block it.

6
00:00:27.168 --> 00:00:32.839
Another runs after a tool returns, where you can clean up the result before Claude reads it.

7
00:00:32.839 --> 00:00:34.442
Think of airport security.

8
00:00:34.442 --> 00:00:36.969
It does not depend on anyone remembering.

9
00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:39.127
Every bag goes through, every time.

10
00:00:40.527 --> 00:00:44.456
A post tool use hook sees the tool's output before Claude does.

11
00:00:44.456 --> 00:00:51.627
Here, we convert every date to the ISO format and map status codes like shipped and in transit onto one fixed list.

12
00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:55.744
We register it with a matcher so it only runs for the order tools.

13
00:00:55.744 --> 00:01:00.047
The model never has to guess what a date like three slash four means.

14
00:01:01.447 --> 00:01:04.076
A pre tool use hook runs before the tool.

15
00:01:04.076 --> 00:01:06.193
This one reads the refund amount.

16
00:01:06.193 --> 00:01:08.437
Anything up to five hundred passes.

17
00:01:08.437 --> 00:01:13.888
Anything above is denied, and the reason tells Claude to escalate to a human instead.

18
00:01:13.888 --> 00:01:15.299
That is the key point.

19
00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:22.033
A hook gives the same answer every time, while a rule in the prompt only makes the right behavior likely.

20
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:31.432
In the support scenario, the orders system and the billing system disagree on dates, and statuses are spelled differently everywhere.

21
00:01:31.432 --> 00:01:34.018
A post tool use hook cleans all of that up.

22
00:01:34.018 --> 00:01:40.152
When the agent tries to refund nine hundred, the pre tool use hook blocks it and points to escalation.

23
00:01:40.152 --> 00:01:43.700
Claude then tells the customer a specialist will take over.

24
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.887
Whenever the question says must, always, or compliance, lean toward a hook over a prompt rule.

25
00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:54.273
Format chaos from several tools is a post tool use job.

26
00:01:54.273 --> 00:01:56.612
And a good block does not just say no.

27
00:01:56.612 --> 00:02:02.337
It gives Claude a reason that leads to the correct path, such as calling the escalation tool.

28
00:02:03.737 --> 00:02:04.284
To recap.

29
00:02:04.284 --> 00:02:08.900
Post tool use hooks clean and reshape tool results before Claude reads them.

30
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.212
Pre tool use hooks inspect calls and block anything that breaks policy.

31
00:02:13.212 --> 00:02:17.524
When you block, give a reason that sends Claude to the right next step.

32
00:02:17.524 --> 00:02:22.383
And whenever compliance has to be guaranteed, choose a hook over prompt wording.

